# 2. Secured and monitored web infrastructure

## What was added

- 3 firewalls, one in front of each machine (load balancer, server 1, server 2)
- 1 SSL certificate for www.foobar.com, so traffic is served over HTTPS
- 3 monitoring clients, one on each machine, shipping metrics and logs to a
  monitoring service (Sumologic, Datadog, Prometheus - the principle is the
  same)

## Why each element was added

**The firewalls** restrict which ports are reachable from where. The load
balancer should accept 80 and 443 from the whole internet; the web servers
should accept traffic only from the load balancer; the database port should
only be reachable from the application servers. Without them, every service on
every machine is exposed to the internet.

**The SSL certificate** lets the site be served over HTTPS so the traffic is
encrypted and the server's identity is verified.

**The monitoring clients** are agents that run on each host, collect metrics
and logs locally, and push them to the monitoring service, which stores them,
graphs them, and raises alerts.

## Specifics

**What are firewalls for**
A firewall filters network traffic against a set of rules, allowing or
blocking packets by source, destination, port, and protocol. It reduces the
attack surface to only the ports that genuinely need to be public, and it
contains the damage if one machine is compromised.

**Why is the traffic served over HTTPS**
HTTPS wraps HTTP in TLS. That gives three things: confidentiality (nobody on
the path can read the traffic), integrity (nobody can modify it undetected),
and authentication (the certificate proves the server really is
www.foobar.com). Without it, passwords, session cookies, and personal data
travel in clear text and anyone on the same network can capture them. Browsers
also flag plain HTTP sites as "Not secure".

**What monitoring is used for**
To know what the system is doing - before, during, and after a problem. It
tracks availability and performance, raises alerts when something crosses a
threshold, gives the data needed to debug an incident, and shows trends so
capacity can be planned ahead of saturation.

**How the monitoring tool is collecting data**
An agent installed on each server reads local sources - CPU, memory, disk and
network counters from the OS, application and web server log files, service
health checks - and pushes that data to the monitoring platform over the
network at a regular interval. The platform aggregates, stores, and visualises
it, and evaluates the alerting rules.

**How to monitor web server QPS**
QPS (queries per second) is derived from the web server's access log. Point
the monitoring agent at Nginx's access log so every request becomes an event,
then build a metric that counts those events per second (or count per minute
divided by 60). Nginx's stub_status module, or an Nginx exporter, can also
expose a request counter that the agent scrapes; the rate of that counter is
the QPS. Then put a dashboard and an alert on it.

## Issues with this infrastructure

**Why terminating SSL at the load balancer level is an issue**
If TLS is decrypted at the load balancer, everything behind it travels in
clear text on the internal network. Anyone who gains a foothold inside the
network - or any misconfigured switch - can read the traffic, so the system is
not truly end-to-end encrypted. The fix is to re-encrypt between the load
balancer and the backends, or to pass the TLS through to them.

**Why having only one MySQL server capable of accepting writes is an issue**
It is a single point of failure and a bottleneck for writes. If the Primary
goes down the application cannot write anything until a replica is promoted,
which usually requires manual intervention and risks losing the transactions
that had not replicated yet. Write throughput also cannot be scaled by adding
replicas.

**Why having servers with all the same components is an issue**
Each server runs the web server, the application server, and a database at the
same time, so they compete for the same CPU, RAM, and disk. A heavy database
query slows down request serving on the same box. It also means the components
cannot be scaled independently - if only the application tier is saturated,
you still have to add a whole stack - and a failure in one component can take
the whole machine with it.
